# L045 — Imported prime-degree period and full-relative symbol transfer

## Hypotheses

Let psi:E0 -> E be a supplied Q-rational isogeny of prime degree ell
between specified global minimal elliptic-curve models over Q. Write
omega_0, omega_E for their specified minimal invariant differentials and
Omega(A)=integral_(A(R))|omega_A| for the total real period. Grant an
independently verified exact pullback

\[
\psi^*\omega_E=d\omega_0,\qquad d\in\mathbf Q^\times.
\]

Grant independent real-map witnesses giving the finite positive integers
k=#ker(psi_R) and c=#coker(psi_R), where the cokernel is the group
E(R)/psi(E0(R)), including all components. Alternatively grant witnesses
meeting the precise prime-degree cases below, sufficient to determine
k/c. An isogeny-class label, the desired normalization, or component
counts alone are not such witnesses. No map, minimality, differential
identity or real-map witness is produced by this conditional result.

Use the common normalized weight-two newform f at their conductor N,
and L028's complete torsion-free integral relative module M with every
oriented integral generator g_i. Grant audited exact values
z_i=F_0(g_i) in Q identifying F_0=I/Omega(E0) on all M, where
I({a,b})=Re(2 pi i integral_a^b f(z) dz). Retain all integral-coordinate
and relation witnesses, not just an absolute-cycle eigenline. One may use
L043 or L044 for the two specified optimal models, retaining their
published optimality qualifications and complete instance audits. More
generally E0 is an independently identified semistable optimal curve with
such audited normalization. No optimality or Manin-constant-one assertion
is imposed on E.

For downstream L027 use, retain separately its non-CM, good-ordinary
p>=5 and primitive ordinary-branch hypotheses and all its working data.
They are not consequences of normalization.

## Conclusion

The exact positive rational period ratio and target normalization are

\[
R=\frac{\Omega(E0)}{\Omega(E)}=\frac{k}{c|d|},\qquad
F_E=\frac I{\Omega(E)}=R F_0
\quad\hbox{on all }M.                                      \tag{1}
\]

In particular S_E(r)=F_E({infinity,r}) in L028's orientation for every
rational r. No extra differential factor enters the common numerator I.

To retain every denominator, write R=A/B with positive coprime integers
A,B. Let D0 be the least common denominator of all z_i, and t_i=D0 z_i
in Z. For each i, including t_i=0 with gcd(0,n)=n, set

\[
h_i=\frac{B D0}{\gcd(|A t_i|,B D0)},\qquad
D_E=\mathop{\rm lcm}_i h_i.                               \tag{2}
\]

Then D_E is the exact least common denominator of all target generator
values A t_i/(B D0), and F_E(M) is contained in (1/D_E)Z. For any prime
p the exact least nonnegative all-module denominator constant is

\[
C_E=\max\bigl(\{0\}\cup
       \{-v_p(R z_i):z_i\ne0\}\bigr)=v_p(D_E).            \tag{3}
\]

Thus v_p(S_E(r))>=-C_E uniformly in r. If C_0 is the corresponding
nonnegative constant for F_0, a conservative bound is
C_E<=max(0,C_0-v_p(R)); exact values in (2)--(3) include cancellation.
Neither C_E=0 nor unchanged denominators are assumed.

Under the cited real-map hypotheses, for odd ell, k/c=ell when the
entire geometric kernel is real, and k/c=1 otherwise. For ell=2, use
an independently justified auxiliary source equation

\[
y^2=x^3+a x^2+b x
\]

with kernel generated by (0,0), b(a^2-4b)!=0, and the transported
isogeny identified with the corresponding degree-two quotient. Then
k/c=1 if b>0 and (a<0 or 4b>a^2), and k/c=2 otherwise. The scalar d
in (1) still uses the specified minimal differentials, not the auxiliary
equation's differential. These are imported cases, not a construction
or verification of the auxiliary change of coordinates.

Conditionally choosing L043 gives F_E=(R/5)phi with D0=10;
choosing L044 gives F_E=-R phi with D0=2. Formula (2) must still be
applied to every generator. No specified target curve or numerical R is
supplied here. This imports known mathematics; it establishes neither
uniform witness production, a coefficient, rational points, Sha finiteness,
complex-order comparison, the refined BSD formula nor the main rank claim.
The separate conditional critical audit of 2026-10-11 passes, with the
odd-degree witness clarification recorded below. No instance is certified.

## Proof

**Published period input.** Import Dokchitser--Dokchitser, *Local
invariants of isogenous elliptic curves*, Trans. AMS 367 (2015),
4339--4358, [arXiv:1208.5519v4 (28 October 2013), Notation 7.1,
Definition 7.2, Remark 7.3, Lemma 7.4 and Proposition 7.6,
pp.15--16](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1208.5519v4#page=15).
Their hypotheses are a prime-degree isogeny in characteristic zero and
nonzero invariant differentials; over R their period integrates the
entire real locus. Our Q-rational map induces exactly this real isogeny,
and the supplied minimal differentials are eligible. Lemma 7.4 gives
Omega(E0)/Omega(E)=(k/c)/|d| in precisely our direction. Proposition
7.6 supplies precisely the odd-prime and degree-two cases just stated.
We import these results without reproof or extension to composite degree.

The essential degree-two component argument cited there was inspected in
Dokchitser--Dokchitser, *Parity of ranks for elliptic curves with a cyclic
isogeny*, JNT 128 (2008), 662--679,
[Section 7.1, p.11 of the arXiv PDF](https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0604149#page=11).
It separates connected target, connected source/disconnected target, and
both disconnected, the last depending on the kernel's component. Its
local Hilbert-symbol setup assumes a!=0. We use the later Proposition
7.6 for the stated full degree-two cases, including a=0; we do not
extend that earlier argument. Real component counts alone do not settle the degree-two quotient.
For odd prime degree, a witnessed nonidentity real kernel point does
suffice: it generates the geometric kernel, so every kernel point is
real. The identity point alone gives no such information.

**Applicability to the numerator.** Rational isogenies identify rational
Tate-module Galois representations: psi and its dual compose to [ell],
which is invertible after tensoring with Q_q at any auxiliary prime q.
Consequently their local Euler data and conductor agree; by modularity
and the standard strong multiplicity-one theorem their associated
normalized newform is the same f. Equivalently its normalization a_1=1
fixes the common form, not a curve-dependent isogeny scale. This is the
usual isogeny-invariance applicability argument, not a new comparison
between complex and p-adic vanishing orders. The supplied normalization
therefore uses exactly the I in the target definition. For every x in M,

\[
F_E(x)=I(x)/\Omega(E)
      =(\Omega(E0)/\Omega(E))F_0(x)=R F_0(x).
\]

This proves equality on the entire relative module using the granted
full-relative identity, rather than extending an equality known only on
absolute cycles. It preserves the orientation and all relations because
it multiplies the entire functional by the single positive rational R.
L028 identifies this oriented integral with lambda^+(r); dividing by
the target's total period proves the asserted symbol convention.

As a source-convention comparison, Wuthrich, *Numerical modular symbols
for elliptic curves*, [arXiv:1608.06423v2, Introduction p.1 equation (1), Section 2 pp.2--3
and Section 2.1 pp.4--5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1608.06423v2#page=1),
retains nonoptimal period ratios but normalizes by the least positive
period Omega+. That convention is not silently substituted here. Both
our source and target denominators are total real periods, even when
component counts differ. No nonoptimal Manin constant is inferred.

**Exact denominator specialization.** The target generator value is
A t_i/(B D0). Cancelling its integer numerator and denominator gives
exactly h_i in (2); a zero value has denominator one. A positive integer
clears every generator value if and only if each h_i divides it, so
D_E is their least common denominator. Every x in M has an integral
expression sum_i n_i g_i by the complete presentation, hence
F_E(x)=sum_i n_i R z_i belongs to (1/D_E)Z. Conversely a uniform
all-module denominator must clear the generators themselves. This
proves the exact claim, not just a product upper bound.

The reduced denominator of a nonzero rational y has p-adic valuation
max(0,-v_p(y)). Taking a least common multiple proves (3). Integral
coefficients and the nonarchimedean inequality then give the bound for
every path; if a generator has negative valuation attaining the minimum,
no smaller nonnegative all-module constant is possible. Since
v_p(R z_i)=v_p(R)+v_p(z_i)>=v_p(R)-C_0, the stated conservative bound
also follows. These are only finite bookkeeping consequences of the
imported ratio and L028's integral-generation argument, not a reproof of
the period theorem. The two optional source normalizations are the
audited conclusions of L043 and L044, with their existing qualifications.

**Scope and continuation test.** An instance can activate (1)--(3) only
with independently verified equations, map direction and prime degree,
nonzero minimal-differential pullback and real-map data meeting a cited
case. Missing any witness parks that instance. Once supplied, evaluate
and reduce every integral-generator value; no new relative-space
construction is necessary. Even then L027 still requires its complete
finite data and strict error threshold above v_p(b), and its other
arithmetic premises remain independent. Historical calibration-production
stops are preserved; this distinct imported period scale cannot produce
the granted isogeny data or solve the rank gap.

**Separate conditional critical audit, 2026-10-11.** Under the prior
SPECIALIZE assessment, the hypotheses, conclusions and complete proof
above were checked against L028 and the already audited L043/L044
source results. The audit passes; it corrects one overbroad explanatory
warning, not the formula or its mathematical inputs.

For the map direction in (1), substitute source E0 and target E in the
cited Lemma 7.4. Its differential term is
|omega_0/(psi*omega_E)|=1/|d|. Thus the source/target period ratio is
k/(c|d|), and division of the common numerator by the target period
multiplies F_0 by R. The source proof integrates over the image and
uses its index c in the entire target real group. This excludes an
additional component factor or a signed d in the period ratio.

Every real case in Proposition 7.6 matches the conclusion: for odd
prime degree the two alternatives are the whole geometric kernel real
or not; for degree two the first branch requires b>0 together with
(a<0 or 4b>a^2), and the complementary nonsingular branch gives two.
The conditions b=0 and a^2=4b are excluded by nonsingularity. The case
a=0 is covered by that proposition, without invoking the earlier
Hilbert-symbol proof outside its scope. Auxiliary equation changes do
not change the requirement that d use the specified minimal differentials.
The former warning about rationality of “just some” odd-degree kernel
points was too broad. In characteristic zero a prime-degree geometric
kernel has prime order. Any nonidentity real point generates it, and
its multiples are real; a nonidentity rational point is a sufficient
special case. This clarifies what a valid independent kernel witness
can establish. No such point or map is supplied in this lemma.

Wuthrich's Introduction explicitly attaches the normalized f to the
isogeny class and uses the integral from infinity to r in equation (1).
The common f is also a hypothesis here. The Tate-module argument is
therefore supporting justification, not a claim to identify a form
from finitely many Hecke values. For an arbitrary integral x in M the
same I(x) occurs on both sides of (1); this proves the full-relative
identity before evaluating generators. No boundary functional or
pullback-differential factor can be added to that fixed definition of I.
The total real period convention is retained for both curves.

For each index i independently, put n=A t_i and m=B D0>0. When n is
nonzero, dividing n and m by gcd(|n|,m) gives coprime numerator and
positive denominator h_i. When n=0, the same formula gives h_i=1.
An integer D clears n/m exactly when h_i divides D. Taking this
condition over every integral generator proves (2), including necessity.
For each prime p, v_p(h_i)=max(0,-v_p(R z_i)) on nonzero values,
and is zero on zero values. The valuation of their lcm is their
maximum, proving (3). Integral generation proves sufficiency on all M;
the generators themselves prove minimality. If every value is p-integral,
minimality among nonnegative constants means C_E=0. The bound involving
C_0 follows from v_p(z_i)>=-C_0 and is only an upper bound, so it does
not suppress cancellation or falsely assert equality when all source
values are divisible by p.

L043 supplies all twelve values and D0=10; L044 supplies all 390 and
D0=2, with their published optimality qualifications. This audit uses
their completed audits and does not replay their certificates. Because
no R is supplied, (2) is verified for every symbolic generator index,
not evaluated as a numerical target certificate. The direct inputs in
the DAG remain L028, L043 and L044; L027 is downstream, not a premise.
No changed argument requires a PROOF.md update. A repeat conditional
audit would not produce the missing instance data. Subsequent production
requires its own source coverage and independent witness verification.

